WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.340 --> 00:00:06.240
Here's some fun math to ruin your day: The chance your vote decides an election is one in sixty million.

2
00:00:06.620 --> 00:00:11.020
The value if your candidate wins is ten thousand dollars (in policies you prefer).

3
00:00:11.520 --> 00:00:16.900
The expected value of voting is zero point zero zero zero one seven dollars.

4
00:00:17.320 --> 00:00:22.040
The cost of voting (time, gas, finding parking) is fifty dollars.

5
00:00:22.120 --> 00:00:26.020
The net value is negative forty-nine dollars and ninety-nine cents.

6
00:00:26.720 --> 00:00:33.900
Voting is literally irrational. You have better odds of dying in a car crash on the way to vote than your vote mattering.

7
00:00:34.320 --> 00:00:38.660
Yet people get really mad when you point this out. Democracy.

8
00:00:39.400 --> 00:00:45.760
The Founding Fathers were brilliant men. They anticipated tyranny, corruption, foreign invasion, and mob rule.

9
00:00:46.160 --> 00:00:52.880
They did not anticipate Florida. Sixty-four percent of voters can't name the three branches of government.

10
00:00:53.900 --> 00:00:58.160
These same voters choose who runs those branches. Not you, of course.

11
00:00:58.240 --> 00:01:04.420
You're reading a book about international treaties. But statistically, the person sitting next to you thinks the judicial

12
00:01:04.420 --> 00:01:12.720
branch is a type of tree. The Rational Ignorance Problem. The cost of being informed is over one hundred hours researching

13
00:01:12.720 --> 00:01:21.000
candidates and issues. The benefit of being informed is your one vote (worth zero point zero zero zero one seven dollars).

14
00:01:21.580 --> 00:01:24.820
The rational decision is to stay ignorant and watch Netflix.

15
00:01:25.720 --> 00:01:30.180
This isn't stupidity. It's the smartest decision your democracy makes possible.

16
00:01:31.000 --> 00:01:36.810
Why spend one hundred hours researching healthcare policy when your vote won't change anything?

17
00:01:37.160 --> 00:01:43.260
You could spend those hours learning Italian, and at least then you'd be able to order gelato while your democracy collapses.

18
00:01:44.280 --> 00:01:52.320
What Democracy Should Look Like vs Reality. Princeton University analyzed one thousand seven hundred seventy-nine policy

19
00:01:52.320 --> 00:01:58.720
decisions over twenty years. The correlation between what ordinary voters want and what actually happens?

20
00:01:59.400 --> 00:02:05.680
ZERO. PERCENT. When economic elites want something? Seventy-eight percent of the time.

21
00:02:05.820 --> 00:02:10.699
You have the same political influence as your houseplant. Slightly less, actually.

22
00:02:10.979 --> 00:02:14.580
Your houseplant produces oxygen, which at least one congressman needs.

23
00:02:15.720 --> 00:02:22.460
Your institutions tell the whole story in their names. You have a "Department of Defense" that mainly attacks people.

24
00:02:22.840 --> 00:02:26.560
A "Department of Health" that has not yet produced any observable health.

25
00:02:26.940 --> 00:02:31.040
And a "Department of Justice" that (you can see where this is going).

26
00:02:31.780 --> 00:02:38.520
Whether zero percent or one hundred percent of voters support a bill, it has the same thirty percent chance of passing.

27
00:02:39.220 --> 00:02:45.420
Democracy is a slot machine where every pull gives the same result but you keep playing because the machine has an American

28
00:02:45.420 --> 00:02:52.180
flag on it. The Mathematics of Political Failure. Concentrated Benefits vs.

29
00:02:52.200 --> 00:02:58.480
Diffuse Costs. Sugar subsidies cost Americans three point five billion dollars per year.

30
00:02:58.920 --> 00:03:03.500
That's ten dollars per person. Will you spend weeks fighting to save ten dollars?

31
00:03:03.900 --> 00:03:09.060
No. Will sugar companies spend millions to keep their three point five billion dollars?

32
00:03:09.600 --> 00:03:18.140
Yes. Guess who wins? This pattern repeats everywhere: Military contractors receive nine hundred ninety-nine billion dollars

33
00:03:18.140 --> 00:03:24.580
in concentrated benefit (thirty-seven percent of two point seven two trillion dollars in global spending).

34
00:03:25.240 --> 00:03:32.300
Taxpayers pay three thousand dollars each in diffuse costs (nine hundred ninety-nine billion dollars divided by three hundred

35
00:03:32.300 --> 00:03:36.860
thirty-three million Americans). The winner is exactly who you would guess.

36
00:03:37.800 --> 00:03:42.560
Pharma companies receive five hundred billion dollars in concentrated profits.

37
00:03:42.560 --> 00:03:46.760
Patients suffer fifteen hundred dollars each in diffuse suffering.

38
00:03:47.540 --> 00:03:54.860
The winner is not you. Banks in 2008 received a seven hundred billion dollar bailout (concentrated).

39
00:03:55.920 --> 00:03:58.610
You paid two thousand one hundred dollars in taxes (diffuse).

40
00:03:59.640 --> 00:04:07.920
The winner is the ones with yachts. How Money Buys Power. Campaign contributions don't directly buy how politicians vote.

41
00:04:08.420 --> 00:04:13.240
Only 1 in 4 studies support that. The system is much more elegant than bribery.

42
00:04:14.240 --> 00:04:18.060
Bribery is what poor countries do. Rich countries have "campaign finance.".

43
00:04:19.040 --> 00:04:23.900
But they do something far more effective: they buy which politicians exist in the first place.

44
00:04:24.940 --> 00:04:30.140
Ninety-five percent of House races since 2004 were won by the highest spender.

45
00:04:30.740 --> 00:04:34.840
The average winning House campaign is two point seven nine million dollars.

46
00:04:35.620 --> 00:04:39.960
The average winning Senate campaign is twenty-six point five three million dollars.

47
00:04:41.200 --> 00:04:46.340
Why bribe a politician when you can just manufacture one who already agrees with you?

48
00:04:46.440 --> 00:04:48.680
It's like complaining about the menu when you own the restaurant.

49
00:04:50.120 --> 00:04:58.060
FOR THE ONES ALREADY IN OFFICE, contributions buy something more subtle than votes: Access: Your call gets returned, theirs

50
00:04:58.060 --> 00:05:03.300
goes to voicemail. Agenda control: Your issue gets a hearing, theirs gets buried.

51
00:05:04.240 --> 00:05:08.220
Legislative language: The exact wording that creates loopholes.

52
00:05:08.920 --> 00:05:15.680
Committee positions: four hundred fifty thousand dollars to party committees buys a seat where bills live or die.

53
00:05:16.580 --> 00:05:22.000
You don't buy the vote. You buy the voter, the agenda, and the room where the vote happens.

54
00:05:23.480 --> 00:05:27.860
The Lobbying R.O.I.: one thousand eight hundred and ten to one Returns.

55
00:05:29.060 --> 00:05:33.680
The most profitable investment in America isn't stocks, real estate, or crypto.

56
00:05:33.700 --> 00:05:38.440
It's congressmen: Disease advocacy groups spend one hundred million dollars on lobbying.

57
00:05:39.180 --> 00:05:42.180
They get one point eight billion dollars in increased funding.

58
00:05:42.740 --> 00:05:52.260
The R.O.I. is one thousand eight hundred and ten to one. Meanwhile: Breast cancer: Great lobbyists result in massive funding.

59
00:05:53.100 --> 00:05:57.260
Heart disease: Kills more people, but worse lobbyists mean less funding.

60
00:05:58.560 --> 00:06:06.720
Rare pediatric diseases: No lobbyists mean no funding. Democracy doesn't allocate resources by who's dying.

61
00:06:07.380 --> 00:06:12.100
It allocates by who can afford the best lobbyist. If your disease can't hire a P.R.

62
00:06:12.620 --> 00:06:19.500
firm, it's not a real disease. It's just dying. Why Nothing Ever Changes: A Mathematical Proof.

63
00:06:20.560 --> 00:06:25.080
The number of Americans who want lower drug prices is three hundred thirty million.

64
00:06:26.000 --> 00:06:33.500
The money they contribute to this cause is zero dollars. The number of pharma executives who want high drug prices is one

65
00:06:33.500 --> 00:06:38.220
thousand. The money they contribute is four point eight eight billion dollars.

66
00:06:39.500 --> 00:06:44.740
One thousand is greater than three hundred thirty million (when you multiply by money).

67
00:06:45.560 --> 00:06:51.620
That's not corruption. That's math. Why Politicians Don't Care About You (With Math!).

68
00:06:52.680 --> 00:06:58.040
A politician needs three things: One. Votes (from idiots). Two.

69
00:06:58.120 --> 00:07:03.060
Money (from corporations). Three. Media coverage (from other corporations).

70
00:07:04.340 --> 00:07:08.640
You provide one one-hundred-and-fifty-millionth of item number one.

71
00:07:09.380 --> 00:07:14.040
Corporations provide one hundred percent of items number two and number three.

72
00:07:14.840 --> 00:07:19.300
Who do you think they listen to? The Iron Law of Oligarchy.

73
00:07:20.080 --> 00:07:23.040
Every democratic organization eventually becomes an oligarchy.

74
00:07:24.060 --> 00:07:30.200
Political parties, unions, non-profits, your homeowners association, the P.T.A.

75
00:07:30.200 --> 00:07:35.210
Sharon from the bake sale committee is running it exactly like a Medici, and she knows it.

76
00:07:35.760 --> 00:07:41.610
Democracy doesn't prevent oligarchy. It gives oligarchy a suit and a certificate that says "elected.".

77
00:07:43.200 --> 00:07:46.900
Your Congressman: A Fundraiser Who Occasionally Legislates.

78
00:07:47.900 --> 00:07:51.760
Average time congressmen spend on: Fundraising: 4 hours a day.

79
00:07:52.300 --> 00:07:57.800
Reading bills: zero hours a day. Actual governing: Whatever's left.

80
00:07:57.840 --> 00:08:05.200
They don't read what they vote on. The PATRIOT Act: 342 pages, voted on 45 minutes after release.

81
00:08:05.780 --> 00:08:10.920
That's seven point six pages per minute. Olympic speed-readers can't do that.

82
00:08:11.440 --> 00:08:16.920
The Affordable Care Act: 2,700 pages, "we have to pass it to see what's in it".

83
00:08:17.720 --> 00:08:24.100
These people control nuclear weapons. Sleep tight. Congressional Committees: The Menu.

84
00:08:24.100 --> 00:08:31.440
Committees control which bills live or die. Both parties assign fundraising quotas for each seat: raise this much for the

85
00:08:31.440 --> 00:08:39.640
party, or lose your assignment. Don't hit your number? Your bills die, your amendments vanish, and your name goes on a literal

86
00:08:39.640 --> 00:08:45.110
"wall of shame". It's like a restaurant where the waiters have to pay for their own sections.

87
00:08:46.060 --> 00:08:51.840
Here's the menu. Want to be House Speaker? Raise twenty to thirty-one million dollars for your party.

88
00:08:52.400 --> 00:08:56.480
Want to chair a powerful committee like Ways and Means or Appropriations?

89
00:08:57.040 --> 00:09:02.360
That'll cost you about one point two to one point eight million dollars in party fundraising.

90
00:09:02.960 --> 00:09:08.900
Even a basic freshman member owes a six-figure fundraising quota just to keep their seat at the table.

91
00:09:09.700 --> 00:09:16.140
The House Speaker position requires raising thirty-one million dollars for the Democratic Congressional Campaign Committee,

92
00:09:16.520 --> 00:09:24.920
or D.C.C.C., and twenty million dollars for the National Republican Congressional Committee, or N.R.C.C., to control the

93
00:09:24.920 --> 00:09:32.360
entire agenda. The Ways and Means Chair costs one point eight million dollars for Democrats and one point two million dollars

94
00:09:32.360 --> 00:09:40.360
for Republicans because all tax laws start here. The Appropriations Chair costs one point eight million dollars for Democrats

95
00:09:40.360 --> 00:09:43.800
and one point two million dollars for Republicans to control a one point seven trillion dollar budget.

96
00:09:43.800 --> 00:09:43.800
Energy and Commerce costs one point eight million dollars for Democrats and one point two million dollars for Republicans

97
00:09:43.800 --> 00:09:50.520
to oversee healthcare and Wall Street. A Regular Committee seat costs between five hundred thousand and one million dollars

98
00:09:50.520 --> 00:09:56.000
for Democrats and eight hundred seventy-five thousand dollars for Republicans; it still beats irrelevance.

99
00:09:57.460 --> 00:10:03.660
A basic freshman member owes a one hundred fifty thousand dollar plus entry fee to play.

100
00:10:04.520 --> 00:10:08.970
This is why congressmen spend 4 hours a day fundraising. They're not campaigning.

101
00:10:09.680 --> 00:10:16.880
They're paying rent. The people writing your healthcare laws literally bought their positions from the industries those

102
00:10:16.880 --> 00:10:21.560
laws regulate. On your planet, this is called "the system working."

103
00:10:22.280 --> 00:10:29.560
The 4-Year Attention Span: Democracy Has A.D.H.D. A politician promises to "cure cancer".

104
00:10:29.960 --> 00:10:35.480
They get elected. They create a blue-ribbon commission. The commission recommends more committees.

105
00:10:35.960 --> 00:10:40.180
The next election happens. A new politician promises to "cure cancer".

106
00:10:40.860 --> 00:10:48.060
This repeats until the heat death of the universe. Democracy has the institutional memory of a concussed goldfish.

107
00:10:48.680 --> 00:10:56.940
Curing cancer takes 20 years. Elections happen every 4. No politician will start something they can't finish and take credit

108
00:10:56.940 --> 00:11:06.340
for. So nothing starts. This is why you have forty-seven consecutive "War on Cancer" declarations and zero cured cancers.

109
00:11:07.360 --> 00:11:11.680
Why No One Can Fix This. Every four years, someone promises to "fix Washington."

110
00:11:13.100 --> 00:11:17.580
Every four years, Washington fixes them. The problem isn't the people.

111
00:11:17.960 --> 00:11:26.000
It's the incentives. You could elect 535 clones of Gandhi and within two years they'd be taking pharma money and approving

112
00:11:26.000 --> 00:11:31.600
bombing campaigns. The system digests idealists like a stomach digests food.

113
00:11:32.080 --> 00:11:34.900
It doesn't matter what you put in. You get the same output.

114
00:11:36.160 --> 00:11:38.740
The Solution Democracy Can't Provide. Democracy can't solve coordination problems.

115
00:11:39.420 --> 00:11:45.900
It is a coordination problem. Example: Climate change. The cost to fix it is one trillion dollars.

116
00:11:46.580 --> 00:11:54.980
The benefit is the continued existence of Miami. The problem is that the benefits are in fifty years, while the costs are

117
00:11:54.980 --> 00:12:00.500
today. The democratic solution is to debate it until the ocean settles the argument.

118
00:12:01.400 --> 00:12:05.740
Example: Medical research. The cost is one hundred billion dollars.

119
00:12:06.320 --> 00:12:13.920
The benefit is to cure all disease. The problem is that the benefits are diffuse, but the costs are concentrated.

120
00:12:15.340 --> 00:12:19.640
The democratic solution is to spend it on a slightly better missile.

121
00:12:20.440 --> 00:12:25.700
Democracy optimizes for short-term concentrated benefits. Every single time.

122
00:12:26.360 --> 00:12:30.180
It's the most reliable machine your species has ever built.

123
00:12:31.380 --> 00:12:39.260
Public Choice Theory: The Nobel Prize for Cynicism. In nineteen eighty-six, James Buchanan won the Nobel Prize in Economics

124
00:12:39.260 --> 00:12:44.160
for formally proving what anyone who has ever been to the D.M.V.

125
00:12:44.160 --> 00:12:49.500
already knew: People don't become angels when you give them a government I.D.

126
00:12:49.660 --> 00:12:57.460
badge. This is Public Choice Theory: bureaucrats, politicians, and regulators are just as selfish as everyone else.

127
00:12:57.940 --> 00:13:02.300
The only difference is that when a corporation is greedy, you can stop buying their product.

128
00:13:02.960 --> 00:13:06.940
When a bureaucracy is greedy, they can arrest you for not buying their product.

129
00:13:08.120 --> 00:13:17.360
What They Actually Maximize. The N.I.H. should maximize cures, but actually maximizes grants awarded, resulting in no cures.

130
00:13:18.080 --> 00:13:25.740
The F.D.A. should maximize lives saved, but actually maximizes avoiding lawsuits, resulting in deadly delays.

131
00:13:26.600 --> 00:13:33.980
Congress should maximize voter welfare, but actually maximizes campaign donations, resulting in corporate welfare.

132
00:13:34.700 --> 00:13:43.950
Pharma should maximize health outcomes, but actually maximizes recurring revenue, resulting in chronic disease being preferred.

133
00:13:44.220 --> 00:13:51.960
Defense contractors should maximize national security, but actually maximize government contracts, resulting in endless

134
00:13:51.960 --> 00:13:56.970
wars. Nobody in the system is incentivized to cure disease.

135
00:13:57.320 --> 00:14:04.520
Or end war. The Libertarian Paradox. Libertarians say that government fails because government is government.

136
00:14:05.620 --> 00:14:09.360
Socialists say that government fails because it needs more government.

137
00:14:10.100 --> 00:14:16.220
Public Choice Theory says that government fails because the people running it are people, and people are terrible.

138
00:14:17.220 --> 00:14:25.300
The solution is to stop expecting people to be good. Design systems where being selfish accidentally produces good outcomes.

139
00:14:26.160 --> 00:14:32.380
Like capitalism, but for not dying. Why This Is Actually Good News.

140
00:14:33.600 --> 00:14:38.180
Democracy can't fix these problems. But something older and dumber can: greed.

141
00:14:39.000 --> 00:14:43.770
Markets don't care if voters are stupid. Markets don't care if politicians are corrupt.

142
00:14:44.080 --> 00:14:50.200
Markets care about one thing: profit. Markets are sociopaths, but reliable sociopaths.

143
00:14:51.040 --> 00:14:55.780
So make the right thing profitable. That's what a one percent treaty does.

144
00:14:56.460 --> 00:15:01.780
It doesn't fix democracy. It routes around it, like the internet routes around damage.

145
00:15:02.600 --> 00:15:11.280
Instead of convincing two hundred forty million voters to be smart (impossible), you convince one thousand investors to

146
00:15:11.320 --> 00:15:18.900
be greedy (they already are). Why This Is Liberating. You don't need virtuous leaders (there aren't any).

147
00:15:19.380 --> 00:15:22.800
You don't need politicians who care (they can't afford to).

148
00:15:23.320 --> 00:15:30.000
You don't need to convince anyone to be less selfish (four thousand two hundred ninety-seven years of data suggest this

149
00:15:30.340 --> 00:15:34.540
doesn't work). You just need to point their selfishness at the right target.

150
00:15:36.120 --> 00:15:40.760
Solution. Democracy fails because: Voters are rationally ignorant.

151
00:15:41.260 --> 00:15:45.840
Politicians follow incentives. Special interests concentrate benefits.

152
00:15:46.420 --> 00:15:54.680
Costs are diffused to everyone. A one percent treaty solution: Don't need informed voters (investors do the math).

153
00:15:55.500 --> 00:16:04.180
Create better incentives (make health profitable). Concentrate benefits for reform (VICTORY Incentive Alignment Bonds).

154
00:16:04.220 --> 00:16:07.980
Diffuse costs to military budget (they won't notice one percent).

155
00:16:09.100 --> 00:16:18.900
You're using democracy's bugs as features. Your Choice. Option A: Keep voting and hope it matters this time (it won't).

156
00:16:19.840 --> 00:16:24.760
Option B: Accept that democracy is broken and use market incentives instead.

157
00:16:25.560 --> 00:16:33.040
The Founding Fathers were geniuses, but they designed a system for four million literate farmers, not three hundred thirty

158
00:16:33.040 --> 00:16:36.620
million people who get their news from a man screaming into a podcast microphone.

159
00:16:38.120 --> 00:16:43.940
Democracy made sense in seventeen eighty-nine. Today, it's a cargo cult with nuclear codes.

160
00:16:44.520 --> 00:16:50.800
But markets? Markets turned feudal peasants into TikTok influencers in four hundred years.

161
00:16:51.460 --> 00:16:58.160
Markets put computers in your pocket more powerful than what landed you on the moon (you use them to argue with strangers

162
00:16:58.160 --> 00:17:02.400
about whether the moon landing was real, but still). Markets work.

163
00:17:03.200 --> 00:17:07.819
You're not making people better. You're building better plumbing for terrible people.

164
00:17:08.300 --> 00:17:11.619
And since everyone's terrible, the plumbing will work perfectly.

165
00:17:12.560 --> 00:17:19.300
Your brain wasn't built for democracy. It evolved to track one hundred fifty people and avoid tigers.

166
00:17:19.920 --> 00:17:25.740
It can't evaluate six trillion dollar budgets or comprehend exponential growth.

167
00:17:26.440 --> 00:17:30.680
For why this matters at the biological level, see Genetic Slavery.

168
00:17:31.400 --> 00:17:35.780
For how the resulting budget process works in practice, see Cost of War.

169
00:17:36.860 --> 00:17:45.140
Regulatory Capture: How Industries Write Their Own Rules. There are thirteen thousand seven hundred registered lobbyists

170
00:17:45.200 --> 00:17:51.580
in Washington. Half of them used to work in the government they're now bribing, which on your planet is called "a career

171
00:17:51.580 --> 00:17:59.280
path" rather than "corruption." In 2023, corporations spent four point one billion dollars on lobbying.

172
00:17:59.900 --> 00:18:06.880
Divide that by five hundred thirty-five members of Congress and you get seven point seven million dollars in professional

173
00:18:07.050 --> 00:18:11.940
persuasion surrounding each congressman who earns less than a mid-level dentist.

174
00:18:12.880 --> 00:18:17.740
The mystery is not that they're corrupt. The mystery is that any of them aren't.

175
00:18:18.680 --> 00:18:23.400
Citizens United made corporations people who can donate unlimited money through Super PACs.

176
00:18:24.280 --> 00:18:30.440
Corporations are people now, which is odd because you can't imprison a corporation, give it chemotherapy, or make it feel

177
00:18:30.440 --> 00:18:34.000
shame. Pharmaceutical companies write their own regulations.

178
00:18:34.820 --> 00:18:38.820
The Medicare Modernization Act banned the government from negotiating drug prices.

179
00:18:39.820 --> 00:18:44.680
That's why insulin that costs six dollars to make costs you three hundred dollars.

180
00:18:45.380 --> 00:18:51.710
A five thousand percent markup. Your street-corner drug dealers find this excessive, and they don't even have shareholders.

181
00:18:52.740 --> 00:19:00.020
The military-industrial complex distributes operations across forty-two states so that cutting any program threatens local

182
00:19:00.020 --> 00:19:03.480
jobs in enough congressional districts to make it impossible.

183
00:19:04.060 --> 00:19:11.460
It's a tumor that grew its own blood supply. The Pentagon has never passed an audit but somehow knows it needs more money

184
00:19:11.460 --> 00:19:16.460
(they can't tell you where the last trillion went, but they're very confident about needing the next one).

185
00:19:17.280 --> 00:19:21.220
Pharma employs three lobbyists for every member of Congress.

186
00:19:21.640 --> 00:19:26.360
The F.D.A. gets seventy-five percent of its drug review budget from the companies it regulates.

187
00:19:27.120 --> 00:19:31.760
For the full accounting, see Cost of War and F.D.A.: Unsafe and Ineffective.

188
00:19:32.420 --> 00:19:39.820
The system maintains itself with the elegant efficiency of a parasite that keeps its host alive just enough to keep feeding.

189
00:19:40.600 --> 00:19:43.980
Cures are a one-time payment; treatments are recurring revenue.

190
00:19:44.640 --> 00:19:52.200
Approving a bad drug ends careers; delaying a good one ends lives (but not careers, and careers are what matter to people

191
00:19:52.240 --> 00:19:56.620
with careers). Everyone who matters wins. Everyone who's dying loses.

192
00:19:57.260 --> 00:20:01.000
The system works perfectly. It's just optimized for the wrong thing.

193
00:20:01.800 --> 00:20:09.320
The Solution They Don't Want. The whole system runs on one trick: each American loses one hundred dollars to corruption

194
00:20:09.400 --> 00:20:17.320
(not worth a phone call). Each corporation gains one hundred million dollars (worth a thousand phone calls, a lobbyist army,

195
00:20:17.460 --> 00:20:23.260
and a senator's vacation home). But what if curing disease was more profitable than treating it?

196
00:20:23.780 --> 00:20:28.400
What if peace paid better than war? That's what a one percent treaty does.

197
00:20:29.140 --> 00:20:32.580
It doesn't fight the corruption machine. It feeds it different inputs.

198
00:20:33.420 --> 00:20:40.340
You're going to out-bribe the bribers with math. P.S. - If this chapter depressed you, good.

199
00:20:40.340 --> 00:20:42.660
Depression is the correct response to your political system.

